<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amun</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amun was an ancient Egyptian god who, combined with the sun god as Amun-Ra, became the chief state deity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. His temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> received vast wealth from royal conquests, and the priesthood of Amun became a major economic and political force, rivalling royal power in the Egyptian Empire's later period. His cult was also central to royal legitimacy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>, centred on the sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Egyptian Report of Wenamun, from about 1075 BCE, shows King Zakar-Baal receiving an envoy of the temple of <b>Amun</b> at Karnak and demanding payment for cedar, consulting the records of his ancestors and treating Egypt as a trading partner rather than an overlord.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The temple complexes, most prominently Karnak and Luxor, functioned simultaneously as religious centres, economic institutions, and state treasuries, accumulating land, livestock, and labour obligations on a scale that eventually made the <b>Amun</b> priesthood a rival power to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The cult of <b>Amun</b>, centred at Jebel Barkal near Napata, played a central role in legitimising royal power, and kings were chosen from among eligible royal kinsmen with the god's oracular approval.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Egyptians worshipped hundreds of gods, the netjeru, who could take human, animal or mixed forms and combine with one another: the sun god Ra merged with the Theban god <b>Amun</b> into Amun-Ra, king of the gods.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars believe the people of Kerma held an animist view of the world, pointing to their treatment of Jebel Barkal as a sacred place, a tradition later taken up by the Egyptians of the New Kingdom, who built a great temple of <b>Amun</b> there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Its hero, an official of the temple of <b>Amun</b> at Karnak sent to buy cedar for the god’s sacred barque, is kept waiting for weeks in the harbour of Byblos, and King Zakar-Baal makes him pay for the timber like any other customer, producing his forefathers’ accounts to…</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
